::: {.doc-subject}

Check if an undirected graph is a cograph.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L34154-L34256){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.is_cograph()
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Is cograph](../assets/graphs/is-cograph.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Left: every node of a, b (shaded violet) is joined to every node of x, y, z (shaded teal), the join of two edgeless sets, so is_cograph() returns True. Right: without the edge a-z, drawn dashed, the nodes z, b, x, a form an induced path on four nodes, in the accent color, so is_cograph() returns False." width="100%"}

A *cograph* (complement-reducible graph) can be built from single nodes by taking
disjoint unions and joins, where a join adds every edge between two graphs.
Equivalently, it has no induced path on four nodes (no $P_4$), and its modular
decomposition (see `modular_decomposition()`) has no prime nodes: every module with
more than one node is disconnected or has a disconnected complement. The tree then
describes the graph completely, which makes many hard problems (cliques, coloring,
independent sets, Hamiltonicity) easy on cographs.

Self-loops and parallel edges are ignored.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   `True` if the graph is a cograph. The empty graph and a single node are.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If the graph is directed.
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~modular_decomposition`) 

:   The tree whose prime nodes this checks for.

## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}

The graph is a cograph exactly when its modular decomposition has no prime node
(Corneil, Lerchs and Stewart Burlingham, 1981), so this runs `modular_decomposition()`
and stops short of building the tree. Linear-time cograph recognizers exist; this
shares the decomposition's cost instead.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

A path on three nodes is a cograph, the join of its middle node with the two ends. A
path on four nodes is the smallest graph that isn't:

::: {#3b47b3d5 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

(
    ec.Graph().add_path_graph(3).is_cograph(),
    ec.Graph().add_path_graph(4).is_cograph(),
)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
(True, False)
```
:::
:::


### Two teams and one link

A lab has two teams, and everyone in a team works with everyone else in it: two
cliques, a union of joins, and so a cograph. When Cy, in the first team, starts working
with Fay, in the second (right), Ada, Cy, Fay and Gil form an induced path on four
nodes, and the graph is no longer a cograph:

::: {#0b8e4996 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
from itertools import combinations

lab = ec.Graph.from_edges(
    [*combinations(["Ada", "Bo", "Cy"], 2),
     *combinations(["Dev", "Eve", "Fay", "Gil"], 2)]
)
lab.is_cograph()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
True
```
:::
:::


